{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787112326,"nonce":"5qjgzFpMYKNC8Idke2SAHRPjKKY=","message":"**Started, and it is being STAGED rather than swept. Stage 1 has landed: `d76ee0d`, on `main`.**\n\n⛔ **This is not a find/replace, and a mechanical sweep of the 961 occurrences would produce garbage.** The ticket deletes a table AND redesigns what replaces it: `notification_policies` gains `group_by` with the collapse computed at delivery, `notifications` moves to a `(conversation_kind, conversation_id)` pair, `channel_threads` gains a generation, nine API paths go as a declared breaking change, and policies gain a subject-kind binding and count-over-window conditions **that do not exist yet**. Renaming the occurrences would leave the design half-built and the tests asserting nothing.\n\n**Stage 1 — done-when 3, the declared prerequisite. LANDED in `d76ee0d`.**\nThe matcher now sees the group's axes widened by the focused alert's own labels. Merged, not replaced, so the change is monotone: every matcher that matched before still matches, and the only new outcomes are matches that were previously impossible to express. That direction matters because the failure mode on this path is a `no_policy` SUPPRESSION — a filter that silently deletes notifications — not an error anyone sees. Asserted by test, both directions.\n\n**The remaining stages, each independently green and pushable:**\n\n| Stage | Done-when | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2 | 2 | `notification_policies.group_by` + collapse at delivery + migration. The test the ticket names: two alerts differing on `alertname`, sharing `node`, land in ONE conversation under `group_by: [node]`. |\n| 3 | 4, 5 | `notifications (conversation_kind, conversation_id)`, retiring `notifications_target_ck` as a special case; `channel_threads.generation` in `threads_subject_uniq`. |\n| 4 | 1, 6 | Drop `alert_groups` and `alert_cases.group_id`; retire `group.opened`/`group.closed` per the 00051/00054 bargain; `internal/grouping` stops owning a table. |\n| 5 | 7 | Nine `/api/v1/alert-groups*` paths, 38 OpenAPI sites, the three `/groups` UI routes, the Slack deep link retargeted at the Case. Declared breaking change. |\n| 6 | 8 | Subject-kind binding + count-over-window conditions, as further axes on the machinery `throttle` and `digest_floor` already are. |\n| 7 | 9, 10, 11 | SPEC §C.3/§C.4 amendments, the superseding ADR carrying the authorised rationale, `CONTEXT.md`, and closing `23303a0`. |\n\nStages 2-4 each need a migration, so the pin in `TestEveryMigrationDownTo00028IsReversible` moves with each one and each needs its own Down assertions.\n\n⚠️ **Done-when 11 says close `23303a0`. `b1e26f7` says that close must be made BY THE OWNER, not an implementer.** It stays open until then, and until this ticket actually lands — closing it earlier would pre-commit a deletion that has not happened.","files":null}]}